    <h1 class="rv in" style="font-size:clamp(2.2rem,5vw,4rem);max-width:20ch">What Should You Not Bring on a Boat Trip in Madeira?</h1>
    <div class="artmeta rv in d1"><span>10 December 2026</span><span>6 min read</span><span>Funchal · Madeira</span></div>
  </div>
</header>

<section class="pad">
  <div class="wrap">
    <article class="article rv">
      <p class="lede">Leave heels, hard luggage, glass, spray sunscreen and any jewellery you would hate to lose at the hotel. On a Chifbay trip food and drinks are already on board, and a drone or anything bulky is worth a WhatsApp question before the day.</p>

      <h2>Why does a small boat change what you should carry?</h2><p>Because everything you bring shares a 9 metre deck with up to five people, sea spray, a swim stop and a fast ride home. On a big ferry a spare bag disappears under a seat. On a private motor boat it is in someone's way for three hours.</p><p>Most packing lists tell you what to add. This one goes the other way. It is the list of things guests regret carrying down the pontoon at Marina do Funchal, with the reason for each, so you can decide for yourself.</p><h2>Which things cause the most trouble?</h2><p>These ten, roughly in order of how often they cause a problem.</p><ol><li><strong>Heels and hard-soled shoes.</strong> You walk a floating pontoon, step across a gap onto the boat and spend the trip on a deck that can be wet. Heels catch, and hard soles slip. Flat sandals with soft rubber soles are fine, and many people end up barefoot on deck.</li><li><strong>Rigid suitcases and big bags.</strong> There is little room for luggage on a boat this size, and a hard case cannot be squeezed into a corner. On a check-out day, leave bags with your hotel's reception before you come, and allow for the walk back to collect them.</li><li><strong>Glass from home.</strong> People walk barefoot after the swim. One dropped bottle and the rest of the trip is about a cut foot.</li><li><strong>Spray sunscreen.</strong> On deck there is almost always a breeze. The American Academy of Dermatology advises against spray sunscreen on windy days, because it is hard to apply and easy to breathe in. Much of it also lands on the seats and the deck instead of your skin, leaving an oily film that people then slip on. Put on cream or lotion at the hotel and bring the tube.</li><li><strong>Jewellery you love.</strong> Rings slip off in cool water, earrings come out when you jump in, and a chain can catch on a ladder. If losing it would ruin your week, it stays in the hotel safe.</li><li><strong>Loose things the wind can take.</strong> A wide hat with no strap, paper napkins, balloons, confetti. The run back to Funchal is fast, and anything light goes overboard and stays in the sea. Bring a hat with a chin strap.</li><li><strong>A cool box full of food and drinks.</strong> Something to eat, island wine, poncha, beer and soft drinks all come with the boat. If you want one special thing, such as a cake or a bottle for an anniversary, message us on WhatsApp first. Read <a href="/posts/food-and-drinks-on-a-private-boat-in-madeira">how bringing your own treats works</a> before you buy anything.</li><li><strong>A stack of layers on a summer Day Trip.</strong> On a July morning a swimsuit, a T-shirt and a towel are enough. The Sunset Trip is different: there, a light jacket is the one layer you should never leave behind.</li><li><strong>Cards and cash you will not use.</strong> A Chifbay trip is paid when you book, and the food and drinks are part of that price. Carry what you need for a taxi or lunch afterwards and leave the rest locked away.</li><li><strong>Electronics you cannot get wet.</strong> A tablet, a laptop for the airport, an expensive camera with no dry bag. Salt spray reaches more places than people think. Your trip is already filmed in 360°, shot from the air by drone when conditions allow and photographed with a real camera, so most groups need no extra gear.</li></ol><h2>Can you bring your own drone?</h2><p>Ask us on WhatsApp first. Every Chifbay trip already includes drone footage from above when the weather allows, so in most cases a second drone adds little and complicates a lot.</p><p>If you still want to fly your own, three things are worth knowing before you pack it:</p><ul><li><strong>The rules apply to visitors too.</strong> Portugal follows the EU drone rules, so most drones with a camera need the operator to be registered, either with ANAC, the Portuguese civil aviation authority, or in another EU country. Portugal has also required a separate authorisation for aerial photos and video. Check both before your holiday, not on the pontoon.</li><li><strong>Some airspace is closed.</strong> The area around Madeira's airport, east of Funchal, is a no-fly zone, and other zones carry conditions. ANAC publishes the maps.</li><li><strong>A boat is a hard place to land.</strong> Taking off from a moving deck with people on it, and catching the drone again, is far harder than on land. A drone that misses the boat is gone for good.</li></ul><h2>What should you carry instead?</h2><p>Very little, which is the whole point. For the Day Trip: swimwear under your clothes, a towel, sun cream that is not a spray, sunglasses on a cord or strap, a hat that ties on, and any medication you might need. For the Sunset Trip: a light jacket or jumper, more so from October to April, and the same sunglasses. Our <a href="/posts/what-to-pack-for-a-boat-trip-in-madeira">item by item list of what to pack</a> covers the rest.</p><p>A phone is fine. Keep it in a pocket that closes or a waterproof pouch, and never hold it over the edge during the swim stop. The guide to <a href="/posts/keep-your-phone-and-camera-safe-on-a-boat-trip">keeping your phone and camera safe on a boat trip</a> has more ideas.</p><p>The <a href="/practical">practical information page</a> keeps the short version in one place, next to the meeting point and the cancellation rules.</p><h2>When is it fine to bring something extra?</h2><p>When you have told us first. It depends on what the item is for, and most reasonable requests are easy if they are planned.</p><ul><li><strong>Medical needs.</strong> Medication, an inhaler, an adrenaline pen or your own food for a severe allergy should always come. Show the skipper where it is when you board.</li><li><strong>A celebration.</strong> A firm cake in a box that closes, a bottle saved for the day. Tell us on WhatsApp so there is a cool, safe spot for it.</li><li><strong>A baby.</strong> Nappies, a change of clothes and a sun hat, of course. Ask on WhatsApp before booking about anything else, such as a life jacket in the right size.</li><li><strong>A dog.</strong> Ask before you book. Do not assume.</li></ul><p>Pack for a swim, a breeze and a few hours on deck, and you will have it right. Everything else either stays at the hotel or starts with a message. If you are still choosing, the <a href="/hidden-coves-half-day">Day Trip with its swim stops</a> and the <a href="/sunset-cruise">Sunset Trip along the cliffs</a> need slightly different bags.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Can you bring your own drinks on a Chifbay trip?</h3><p>You rarely need to, since wine, poncha, beer and soft drinks come with the boat. For a bottle saved for the occasion, ask on WhatsApp first.</p><h3>What shoes should you wear on a boat in Madeira?</h3><p>Flat sandals or trainers with soft rubber soles for the pontoon. Leave heels and hard soles behind. Bare feet on deck are common.</p><h3>Is spray sunscreen a bad idea on a boat?</h3><p>Usually, yes. The wind blows most of it away or onto the deck, and it is easy to breathe in. Use cream or lotion instead.</p><h3>Where do you leave luggage on a check-out day?</h3><p>With your hotel's reception, before you walk to the marina. There is little room for suitcases on a small boat.</p>

      <blockquote>If the wind could take it or the sea could keep it, it stays at the hotel.</blockquote>
    </article>
